{"run_id": "v2-edd2f6c476fb628b90bcd1cf", "question_id": "sciq-train-00797", "condition_id": "P1R1_combined", "student_level": "advanced", "generator_model": "meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-Turbo", "schema_version": "0.1", "status": "answered", "answer": {"schema_version": "2.0", "final_choice": null, "explanation": "The tidal forces near a black hole are so great they can actually tear matter from a companion star due to the extreme gravitational force and rapid variation of the gravitational force near the black hole.", "citations": ["openstax_college_physics_2e__89ad46e1222dbb4a__6__e924c191__00388", "openstax_college_physics_ap_2e__919d8eba53f94d04__6__e924c191__00380", "openstax_college_physics_2e__89ad46e1222dbb4a__34__e924c191__02340", "openstax_college_physics_ap_2e__919d8eba53f94d04__34__e924c191__02306", "openstax_college_physics_2e__89ad46e1222dbb4a__6__e924c191__00387"], "abstained": false}, "error": null}
{"run_id": "v2-9a4f4c275f73a0b8dc7bbe52", "question_id": "sciq-train-01931", "condition_id": "P0R0_plain", "student_level": "beginner", "generator_model": "meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-Turbo", "schema_version": "0.1", "status": "answered", "answer": {"schema_version": "2.0", "final_choice": "A", "explanation": "Radiotherapy is effective against cancer because cancer cells reproduce rapidly and, consequently, are more sensitive to radiation.", "citations": ["openstax_physics__68184df67ec9780c__22__e924c191__01218", "openstax_college_physics_2e__89ad46e1222dbb4a__32__e924c191__02230", "openstax_college_physics_ap_2e__919d8eba53f94d04__32__e924c191__02197", "openstax_college_physics_2e__89ad46e1222dbb4a__32__e924c191__02231", "openstax_college_physics_ap_2e__919d8eba53f94d04__32__e924c191__02198"], "abstained": false}, "error": null}
{"run_id": "v2-763418a383f4e4c6c8fa0f2e", "question_id": "sciq-train-05409", "condition_id": "P1R0_prompt_only", "student_level": "intermediate", "generator_model": "meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-Turbo", "schema_version": "0.1", "status": "answered", "answer": {"schema_version": "2.0", "final_choice": null, "explanation": "The governing principle is the law of conservation of charge, which states that the total amount of charge is always constant. The evidence shows that when charges are separated, existing charges are moved about, but no new charge is created or destroyed. This is demonstrated in the discussion of charging by friction, where the transfer of negative charge from one material to another results in the other material becoming positively charged. The law of conservation of charge is universally obeyed and is a fundamental principle of physics.", "citations": ["openstax_physics__68184df67ec9780c__18__e924c191__00877", "openstax_physics__68184df67ec9780c__18__e924c191__00879", "openstax_college_physics_2e__89ad46e1222dbb4a__18__e924c191__01179", "openstax_college_physics_ap_2e__919d8eba53f94d04__18__e924c191__01160", "openstax_college_physics_2e__89ad46e1222dbb4a__19__e924c191__01244"], "abstained": false}, "error": null}
{"run_id": "v2-5cbe66626b473841fcf6316a", "question_id": "sciq-train-09086", "condition_id": "P1R1_combined", "student_level": "intermediate", "generator_model": "meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-Turbo", "schema_version": "0.1", "status": "answered", "answer": {"schema_version": "2.0", "final_choice": "A", "explanation": "The pion can only be created by violating the conservation of mass-energy, as explained by the Heisenberg uncertainty principle.", "citations": ["openstax_college_physics_2e__89ad46e1222dbb4a__33__e924c191__02266", "openstax_college_physics_ap_2e__919d8eba53f94d04__33__e924c191__02232", "openstax_college_physics_2e__89ad46e1222dbb4a__33__e924c191__02267", "openstax_college_physics_ap_2e__919d8eba53f94d04__33__e924c191__02233", "openstax_physics__68184df67ec9780c__23__e924c191__01234"], "abstained": false}, "error": null}
{"run_id": "v2-7497d63d21f69d32d60ef0a2", "question_id": "sciq-train-09217", "condition_id": "P1R0_prompt_only", "student_level": "beginner", "generator_model": "meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-Turbo", "schema_version": "0.1", "status": "answered", "answer": {"schema_version": "2.0", "final_choice": null, "explanation": "Periodic motion is a motion that repeats itself at regular time intervals, such as exhibited by a vibrating guitar string. The time to complete one oscillation remains constant and is called the period T. Its units are usually seconds, but may be any convenient unit of time.", "citations": ["openstax_college_physics_2e__89ad46e1222dbb4a__16__e924c191__01047", "openstax_college_physics_ap_2e__919d8eba53f94d04__16__e924c191__01030", "openstax_physics__68184df67ec9780c__5__e924c191__00293", "ck12_peoples_physics_concepts__d976bbd43160dfdf__10__e924c191__00098", "openstax_college_physics_2e__89ad46e1222dbb4a__16__e924c191__01038"], "abstained": false}, "error": null}
